  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nTonight through Saturday...The main focus during this forecast \nperiod remains that of the oppressive heat that'll be observed over \nthe area through at least the middle of the week. Strong high \npressure aloft remains wedged into the region from the west, while \nhigh pressure at the surface remains wedged in from the south.  The \ncombination of both will yield a period of above normal temperatures \n(highs from the mid 90s to just above the century mark), along with \nvery humid conditions as dew points sit in the 70s. Afternoon heat \nindex values will consistently climb to & above 106F through mid-\nweek, with some locations along & west of the Interstate 55 corridor \nseeing indices climb to and above 110F.\n\nA \"Heat Advisory\" remains in effect across the forecast area this \nafternoon and Monday as indices range between 106F-110F. The \nexception to this is currently across Bolivar, Sunflower, & Leflore \ncounties where afternoon heat index values will climb above 110F. \nHere, an \"Extreme Heat Warning\" is in effect today & Monday. In \naddition on this afternoon's package, confidence in indices climbing \nabove 110F has increased across a larger portion of the CWA for \nTuesday. Therefore, we've expanded the \"Extreme Heat Warning\" over \nsome central and western portions of the area Tuesday, with a \"Heat \nAdvisory\" in effect elsewhere.\n\nAt the end of the forecast period, Thursday through Saturday, the \naforementioned high aloft will weaken its presence across region as \nit retreats westward.  This will allow troughing along the east \ncoast to deepen across the Southeast United States.  As this \nhappens, another surface boundary will attempt to sink south into \nthe CWA. This will increase rain chances across the area, \npotentially bringing at least some temporary relief to the heat. One \nthing of note during this time, while chances for isolated to \nscattered convection will increase, the increased northwest flow \naloft across the region could yield an MCS or two affecting the \nforecast area. This is still many days out and will continue to be \nmonitored in future model data, as well as in radar and satellite \ndata as we get into that Thursday-Saturday timeframe. /19/",
